WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.630
Film Courage: Would you say screenwriting
is hard?

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:05.780
Because I’m sure people can look at your
story, they could look at your credits, you

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:09.720
make it look easy in terms of “Wow, that’s
a really cool story.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:16.650
Here you were age 14 and you send this script
idea off and it’s rejected and you don’t

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.770
know what the notes are but you still have
this relationship that really shaped you in

00:00:21.770 --> 00:00:26.359
a lot of ways and a lot of people probably
had that opportunity [but didn’t follow

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:27.359
up on it].

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:28.600
But is it hard?

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:32.310
It looks easy from an outsider's view.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.840
Jeffrey Reddick, Horror Writer: It’s hard.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:41.790
It’s always interesting because sometimes
if I have a story that I love and I’ve outlined

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:44.130
it, then it’s just fun.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:49.469
But again when you’re definitely trying
to do a lot of things and get a lot of things

00:00:49.469 --> 00:00:54.510
done because you know I’m at a point in
my career where I want to branch out and still

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:59.760
tell horror stories but do something like
the Lionsgate project which I think is a really

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.920
important kind of thing where you had to fight
for it.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:10.130
It is hard because you want to write something
that is going to strike a chord with people

00:01:10.130 --> 00:01:11.810
but you want to write something that is successful.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.110
You want to write something that is going
to spawn a franchise.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.369
For better or for worse some of my films have
done really well.

00:01:18.369 --> 00:01:21.939
And other ones that were supposed to turn
out well that didn’t turn out the way they

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:26.659
were supposed to and went direct-to-video
or didn’t get a good release.

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:35.759
So you definitely have to stay in gratitude
a lot of times and you have to find the joy

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:38.249
in it all, the joy in writing.

00:01:38.249 --> 00:01:43.829
And I still love writing specially if I’m
writing something else and something inspires

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:51.060
me…I hate to say like a kill scene but you
know I just thought of a great scene for a

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:55.619
movie that I was so excited and I called the
producer about it and he was in love with

00:01:55.619 --> 00:01:56.619
it.

00:01:56.619 --> 00:01:59.679
So that’s the fun stuff, trying to come
up with new things.

00:01:59.679 --> 00:02:05.859
It is hard to sit down in front of a computer
everyday and writing a 100-page script it

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:12.870
is work and it’s not easy because you’re
trying to write something that is going to

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:15.969
be creatively satisfying, you’re trying
to write something that you think will sell

00:02:15.969 --> 00:02:18.530
because this is your livelihood.

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:23.640
So you’re trying to juggle both of those
things but not get too far down the road that

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.150
you’re not focused on finishing the actual
script so you’ve got to try to keep all

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.930
those other things out of your head about
is this going to sell, is this going to make

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.711
money, are people going to like this?

00:02:33.711 --> 00:02:36.849
Try not to focus too much on that and just
get the script done.

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:42.120
But that can be hard in and of itself, not
future-tripping about what’s going to happen

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.750
with the script once you’re done with it.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:49.200
It’s fun but it’s not easy.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.849
And there are harder…you know it’s like
with actors because acting is not easy, to

00:02:53.849 --> 00:02:55.049
be a good actor is not easy.

00:02:55.049 --> 00:03:02.530
But it’s not like we’re putting our fires
and putting our lives in danger and doing

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:10.079
psychically hard labor, it’s just mental
exhaustion (a lot of mental work).

00:03:10.079 --> 00:03:15.920
Film Courage: Did the screenwriting career
that you envisioned look the same in terms

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:16.920
of the day-to-day?

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.780
Because you know how people have a romantic
notion about what something is?

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:30.110
Jeffrey: No, not at all because you don’t
really envision with the writing…you don’t

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.940
realize how long it takes to write a script
first of all.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:37.579
And then I had a friend once who was like
“Oh yeah I just took a weekend trip to Hawaii

00:03:37.579 --> 00:03:39.540
and wrote my script on the beach with some
Mai Tais.”

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:40.671
And I’m like “Oh that’s what writing
is like.

00:03:40.671 --> 00:03:42.680
You just go out for a weekend and you write
a script.”

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:48.700
Yeah, no the day-to-day grind of getting two
or three or four or five pages a day done,

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.849
it’s a grind and then you’ve also got
life that you’re living and you’ve also

00:03:52.849 --> 00:03:56.420
got to try and get out to have a life.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:04:02.190
I’m kind of joking because writers are very
solitary but I do go out to the movies and

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:05.020
I do go out to dinner and I do go out and
do stuff with my friends because otherwise

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:10.349
if you sit at home all the time you’re not
experiencing life, you can’t bring anything

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:14.579
else to your script than what you’ve experienced
so you’re just kind of regurgitating the

00:04:14.579 --> 00:04:16.599
same experiences that you’ve already had.

00:04:16.599 --> 00:04:21.519
You’ve got to make sure that your cup is
flowing over with other things besides just

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.160
work, you have to take time out to do that
too.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.919
Film Courage: In terms of what most people
would envision like you’re at the keyboard

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:34.921
and got a…well not a drink…but some people
they think that is what is part of [the writing

00:04:34.921 --> 00:04:38.500
process] with the cigarette and just like
this tortured existence and you’re pounding

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:41.210
out your beautiful story.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:44.620
But it sounds like there is more of a science
with it?

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:49.360
Jeffrey: There’s a science but for some
people (again everybody has a different method),

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:55.820
it’s like with acting you have your method
actors, they have to become the character.

00:04:55.820 --> 00:05:03.750
You have your other actors that when the director
says “Rolling” then they go and then “Cut!”

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.030
And they drop back into who they are.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.440
And I think there are writers who are the
same way too.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.220
There’s that stereotype, that’s why I
quit drinking I was becoming that stereotype,

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.770
sitting here drinking at home and thinking
I’m writing some brilliant stuff.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.610
And yeah, the first couple pages were great
and then the rest of it is “What the hell

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:23.180
a I writing?”

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:28.199
I do have some friends that do torture themselves
without the drinking or anything.

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:32.129
They obsess about every single line of dialogue
and it’s like you’re never going to finish

00:05:32.129 --> 00:05:35.830
your script if you are like that but again
that’s how they are.

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.449
So everybody got a whole different process.

00:05:37.449 --> 00:05:42.620
That’s why I always tell people don’t
judge yourself harshly if your process isn’t

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:46.490
the same as somebody else’s because it really
is your end result that matters.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.460
It’s getting a script finished.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:53.950
You do obviously have to follow certain structural
rules and your dialogue needs to be good,

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:59.410
it has to be an engaging story but sometimes
it takes people a lot longer.

00:05:59.410 --> 00:06:02.840
I’ve know friends where it’s taken then
10 years to write a script and it’s not

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:05.310
like they are working every day, 8 hours a
day on it.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:06.830
Film Courage: 10 years, wow.

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:10.190
Jeffrey: Yeah I have a couple of friends who
are “ I’ve been working on this for 10

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.190
years.”

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:14.030
And some of their stuff is great and I’ve
read some scripts where people have written

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:22.010
a script in a week and it’s great, not consistently
but everybody has got a different thing and

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:25.060
I think that’s always interesting too is
we always have our ideas in our head of what

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:32.750
everybody’s life is like and even what you
think an actor’s life is like their lives,

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:38.410
the celebrities lives are trudging into Starbucks
with their dog and their kid is crying and

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:42.409
they have to go change their kids diaper,
they are just like everybody else’s.

00:06:42.409 --> 00:06:48.420
You definitely have the glamour of Hollywood
that used to project and was so important

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:54.259
back in the days, the olden days with the
Hollywood stars where the stars ran everything

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:56.340
and they have to give them these perfect images.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:00.960
We’ve lost all of that with the Internet
and access to everybody’s private lives

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.830
and we see people now on the street and people
are taking pictures of people “And oh wait

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.759
a minute, they are just like us.”

00:07:07.759 --> 00:07:10.170
I think People had a headline “Celebrities
Are Just Like Us.”

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:15.210
And it shows pictures of them walking a dog
before that you thought they were these magical

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:21.530
creatures and you thought these writers were
like tortured sitting in a dark room just

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:28.159
pounding away at the keyboard and sipping
from their bottle of Whiskey and cursing the

00:07:28.159 --> 00:07:29.159
heavens.

00:07:29.159 --> 00:07:32.289
It’s different for everybody, it’s different
for everybody.

00:07:32.289 --> 00:07:35.680
But they are all at Starbucks.

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.479
Coffee shops are full of writers in California.

00:07:37.479 --> 00:07:42.599
That’s definitely one cliche that’s true.

00:07:42.599 --> 00:07:46.551
Every coffee shop there are five people working
on a screenplay at any coffee shop that you

00:07:46.551 --> 00:07:48.580
walk into, so it’s pretty funny.

